course: english
lesson: 6
title: Spelling plurals: -s, -es, -ies, -ves and odd ones
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Plural means more than one; singular means just one («singular» has «single» inside it). Most nouns just add -s: cat, cats. But after s, x, z, ch and sh you add -es, because you can hear an extra beat: fox-es, box-es, church-es. Say the plural aloud: if there is a new beat, it is -es; that is why «foxs» looks wrong to Sam's sister.
explain: Words ending in y look at the letter before it. A consonant before the y: change y to i and add -es (baby, babies; party, parties). A vowel before the y: just add -s (day, days; toy, toys). Changing every y would give monsters like «dayies».
explain: Many words ending in -f or -fe turn into -ves: leaf, leaves; loaf, loaves; knife, knives; wolf, wolves; half, halves. Not all do: roof, roofs; chef, chefs. Some words ending in o add -es (potatoes, tomatoes, heroes, echoes), others just -s (pianos, photos, zoos); a silly sentence helps: «The heroes ate potatoes and tomatoes in the echo.»
explain: Some of the oldest, commonest words change inside instead of adding s: mouse, mice; man, men; foot, feet; tooth, teeth; child, children. Some do not change at all: one sheep, ten sheep; one deer, two deer. Words borrowed from Latin may keep an old plural (cactus, cacti; fungus, fungi), and often the English one is fine too (cactuses).
explain: An apostrophe never makes a plural. «The teachers were happy» needs no apostrophe; «the teacher's desk» means the desk of one teacher. A wrong apostrophe in a plural is so common on shop signs («apple's 50p») that it has a nickname, the greengrocer's apostrophe.
fact: Singular means one; plural means more than one.
fact: Most nouns add -s for the plural: cat, cats.
fact: Nouns ending in s, x, z, ch or sh add -es, which adds an extra beat: foxes, boxes, churches, wishes.
fact: Consonant + y changes to -ies (baby, babies); vowel + y just adds -s (day, days).
fact: Many -f and -fe words change to -ves (leaf, leaves; knife, knives; loaf, loaves), but some just add -s (roofs, chefs).
fact: Some -o words add -es (potatoes, tomatoes, heroes, echoes) and others add -s (pianos, photos, zoos).
fact: Some old common words change their vowel or ending: mouse/mice, man/men, foot/feet, tooth/teeth, goose/geese, child/children.
fact: Some nouns are the same in the plural: sheep, deer, moose, aircraft, and usually fish.
fact: Some words from Latin have old plurals (cactus/cacti, fungus/fungi); the English plural is often accepted too.
fact: An apostrophe is never used to make a plural; «teacher's» means belonging to one teacher.
fact: Slang words follow the ordinary plural rules (arvo, arvos), but school writing uses the standard word (afternoons).
fact: Slang in this lesson: «arvo» is Australian for afternoon; «no cap» means «no lie, for real»; «wee» is Scottish for small; «mate» means friend.
exercise: Sam writes «foxs». What is the right plural? | foxes | foxies | foxs' | fox's
exercise: Why does «box» take -es? | You can hear an extra beat: box-es | It ends in a vowel | Every short word adds -es | Because box is a word from French
exercise: Lily writes «babys». What is the rule? | Consonant before y: change y to i and add -es | Always add -s to words ending in y | Vowel before y: change y to i and add -es, as in «dayies» | Words ending in y never change at all
exercise: What is the plural of «day»? | days | daies | dayes | dayies
exercise: Jamie in Glasgow says «two wee loafs». What does his teacher write? | loaves | loafes | loafs | loavs
exercise: Which -f word just adds -s? | roof | leaf | wolf | half
exercise: Ruby says «mouses». What is the plural of «mouse»? | mice | meese | mices | mouses
exercise: Theo writes «sheeps». What does «no cap, sheep is sheep» mean? | For real, the plural is also «sheep» | Take off your cap before you write | Sheep need no capital letter | Sheep always takes a capital S
exercise: Maya writes «potatos». What is right? | potatoes | potatos | potatoe's | potatice
exercise: Which -o word just adds -s? | piano | tomato | hero | echo
exercise: Zoe's dad in Melbourne says «two arvos a week». What is an «arvo»? | An afternoon | A swimming lesson | A sports car | A week
exercise: Harry writes «The teacher's were happy». What is wrong? | A plural needs no apostrophe: teachers | It needs two apostrophes: teacher's' | Teachers should have a capital T | Nothing is wrong with it
exercise: Amir sees «cacti» on one label and «cactuses» on the next. Which is wrong? | Neither, both are accepted | «Cactuses», because cactus is Latin | «Cacti», because English adds -s | Both, the plural is «cactusses»
exercise: Ellie thinks «plural» means «plus». What does it mean? | More than one | Adding two numbers | Just one | A kind of plum
exercise: What is the plural of «child»? | children | childs | childes | childrens
exercise: Which word means just one? | singular | plural | single-plural | many
test: «mate, it's 'two fishes and three sheeps', sounds right innit». Put him right. | Usually «fish» and always «sheep»: they stay the same | He's right, every noun adds -s or -es | It should be «fishs» and «sheepes» | It should be «fishies» and «sheepies»
test: «baby changes to babies so day must change to dayies». What is the slip? | «Day» has a vowel before y, so it just adds -s: days | Nothing, «dayies» is right | It should be «daies», not «dayies» | «Day» has no plural at all
test: A greengrocer's sign says «apple's 50p». What should it say? | apples 50p | apple's' 50p | apples' 50p | Apple's 50P
test: «every f word changes to ves, so it's rooves and cheves». What is true? | Many change, but «roofs» and «chefs» just add -s | Every word ending in f changes to -ves | No f word ever changes to -ves | Only animal words change to -ves
test: What would happen if we wrote «box» plural as «boxs»? | The spelling would not show the extra beat we hear | Nothing, «boxs» is just as correct | The word would mean one box | It would be the American spelling
test: In a cartoon, Gran says «two mouses ran past my two foots!». How should she say it? | Two mice ran past my two feet | Two mices ran past my two feets | Two mouse ran past my two foot | Two mouses ran past my two feet
test: Zoe asks if «arvos» is a real plural. What is the best answer? | Yes, slang follows plural rules, but write «afternoons» at school | No, slang words can never be plural | Yes, and use it in every school essay | No, the plural is «arvoes», like potatoes and tomatoes
test: «potatoes, tomatoes, so it's photoes and pianoes too, sound». What is true? | Those two just add -s: photos and pianos | He's right, all words that end in o add -es in the plural | All o-words just add -s, even potatos | Only food words end in -o
test: «is it one deer, two deers?» What is the plural? | deer | deers | deeres | dear
test: ★★★ How many of these plurals are spelled correctly: wolves, roofs, babies, foxs? | 3 | 4 | 2 | 1
test: ★★★ Which word has a plural that adds -es AND changes its last letter? | lady | dish | key | cat
test: ★★★ «Two teeth, two feet, two geese.» What is the plural of «goose» and of «moose»? | geese and moose | geese and meese | gooses and moose | geese and mooses only
exam: Ascertain the correct plural form of a noun terminating in a consonant followed by the letter y. | The y is replaced by i and the ending -es is appended | The ending -s is appended without alteration of the word | The y is doubled before the ending -es is appended | The word remains unaltered in the plural
exam: Identify the principal reason for which nouns terminating in s, x, z, ch or sh take the ending -es. | The plural thereby acquires an additional syllable which is audible in speech | Such nouns are invariably of French origin and retain the French manner of forming the plural | The ending -es denotes possession in such nouns | Such nouns are always verbs in disguise
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the use of the apostrophe in plural nouns. | The apostrophe is not employed in order to form the plural of a noun | The apostrophe is required in every plural noun | The apostrophe is required in plural nouns which terminate in s | The apostrophe is required in plurals displayed upon shop signs
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the plural of «sheep»? | The plural is identical in form to the singular | The plural is formed by the addition of -s | The plural is formed by the alteration of the vowel | The plural is formed by the addition of -en
